  "form": "Problem note",
  "pages": 21,
  "question": "Why does the cubic rate a_n²/a_{n+1}=1+3/n+o(n^{-3}) force an irrational reciprocal sum?",
  "precis": "For strictly increasing positive integers with a_n²/a_{n+1}=1+3/n+o(n^{-3}), the reciprocal sum is irrational. The argument extracts a cubic polynomial from an integer tail numerator, restricts its coefficients using a square in a cubic field, and excludes the remaining cases modulo seven. The unrestricted Erdős–Graham question remains open.",
  "owns": "the cubic-rate irrationality theorem and its proof; the long paper develops the separate Sylvester-tail criteria and broader recurrence questions",
  "not_authority_for": "the validity of claims tagged Lean, which belongs to the cited kernel-checked source, or a solution to Erdős #243, which remains open",
